    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1937876549669118340",
      "url": "https://x.com/ChiefEngineerCE/status/1937876549669118340",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/ChiefEngineerCE/status/1937876549669118340",
      "text": "Built by Americans. Captured by Foreigners.\n\nPost #5\nPhase Four: The Offshore Exodus ... How American Jobs Were Moved, Not Just Replaced\n\nYou’ve seen the hiring shift. You’ve seen the leadership change. You’ve seen Americans benched, sidelined, and silenced.\n\nNow comes the final blow.\n\nOffshoring.\n\nOnce the internal team is stacked and the culture has shifted, the next step is to remove the jobs altogether.\n\nWe aren’t guessing. This is documented, deliberate, and devastating.\n\nSouthern California Edison.\nHundreds of American IT workers were told their roles were being transitioned. They were forced to train their H‑1B replacements, workers imported from India through outsourcing firms like Infosys and Tata. One employee said:\n\n\"We felt like we were digging our own graves.\"\n\nSome even had to document every step of their job, only to be laid off the next week.\n\nDisney.\nOne of the most iconic American companies did the same. IT staff were told their jobs were ending, but first, they needed to train their replacements, foreign contractors brought in on temporary visas. Congressional testimony detailed the humiliation:\n\n\"They called it ‘knowledge transfer.’ We called it betrayal.\"\n\nAnd still, no apology. No reversal- except there is good news hidden in citations!  Disney executives called it a business decision.\n\nIBM.\nOnce the pride of American innovation, IBM shifted over 9,000 jobs to India. Internal memos described the transition as cost-effective. Entire teams were moved offshore, often without notifying U.S. counterparts until after the fact.\n\n“We logged in one morning and the project was gone. The team was gone. Nobody had told us.\"\n\nBank of America.\nHundreds of tech and support roles were moved to India and the Philippines. The workers in Charlotte and Dallas were let go in waves. Each round quieter than the last. Whistleblowers described the mood as funereal.\n\n“Every Friday felt like a coin toss. You didn’t know if your badge would work on Monday.”\n\nComcast.\nInsiders report telecom jobs increasingly shifted to offshore call centers and tech support hubs. Job postings in the U.S. dry up while similar roles appear in India and the Philippines. Customers complain of declining service. Workers describe a silent purge.\n\nUnited Healthcare.\nInternal presentations revealed plans to legally reclassify and offshore U.S. tech roles in waves. Reports from IT staff suggest departments were broken up, tasks reassigned, and final stages routed overseas.\n\nTesla.\nDespite its American branding, recent reports allege Tesla laid off thousands of U.S. workers while increasing H‑1B hiring. Critics argue many roles could have stayed domestic, but visa access made offshoring cheaper and easier.\n\nThis is not adaptation. It’s extraction.\n\nJobs that once paid $100K in the U.S. are now done for $20K abroad.\n\nAnd it wasn’t just about saving money, it was about stealing it.\n\nA 2023 Economic Policy Institute report found widespread wage theft in the H‑1B program. Companies often underpaid foreign workers compared to U.S. wage standards, and by doing so, they not only displaced American workers but exploited the replacements.\n\nThis wasn’t innovation. It was exploitation... on both sides.\n\nCompanies cut costs, paid less than market rates, and pocketed the difference, all while claiming to be “diversifying” or “reskilling.”\n\nBut here’s the real kicker... the American taxpayer still subsidizes it.\n\nVisa programs are federally funded. Outsourcing firms receive U.S. contracts. And the companies that fire Americans get tax breaks for restructuring.\n\nLet that sink in: You paid for your own replacement.\n\nAnd while your income disappeared, Indian media outlets celebrated.\n\n“India to receive record $120 billion in remittances from global workforce.” “Indian outsourcing firms land major contracts with U.S. giants.” “H‑1B visa program critical to India’s growth strategy.”\nTheir success depends on your silence.\nBecause if Americans really knew what was happening... they’d revolt.\n\nBut here’s the pattern:\n\nGet Americans to build it.\n\nBring in foreign leadership.\n\nShift hiring to visa holders.\n\nTrain replacements.\n\nMove the whole department abroad.\n\nYou’re not being outcompeted. You’re being targeted.\n\nComing next: Phase Five... the government and global strategy behind your replacement.\n\nThis is Post #5 in the “Built by Americans. Captured by Foreigners.” series.\nFollow to see Post #6... how India made this a national strategy.\n\nIf this one opened your eyes, share it.\nIf you’ve felt it firsthand, bookmark it.\nAnd if you want this truth to reach more people, subscribe for a dollar.\n\nPost #6 drops after 100 likes or 20 shares.",
